  "id": "container-with-most-water",
  "title": "Container With Most Water",
  "track": "Two Pointers",
  "difficulty": "medium",
  "topics": ["two-pointers"],
  "source": "canonical Container With Most Water concept; description, examples, and tests re-authored",
  "description": "The list heights gives one vertical line per index: line i rises from 0 up to heights[i]. Choose two lines; with the ground they form a container that holds water up to the height of the shorter line, and the water area is the distance between the two indices times that shorter height. Return the largest water area any pair of lines can hold.",
  "constraints": [
    "2 <= len(heights) <= 20000",
    "0 <= heights[i] <= 10000"
  ],
  "parameters": ["heights"],
  "functionName": "max_area",
  "starterCode": "def max_area(heights):\n    # Return the largest area: (distance between indices) * (shorter height).\n    ...\n",
  "examples": [
    {
      "input": { "heights": [3, 1, 2, 4, 3] },
      "output": 12,
      "explanation": "lines 0 and 4 hold 4 x 3 = 12"
    },
    {
      "input": { "heights": [2, 2, 2] },
      "output": 4,
      "explanation": "lines 0 and 2 hold 2 x 2 = 4"
    }
  ],
  "hints": [
    "The area is limited by the shorter of the two lines, so think about which line is worth moving.",
    "Start from the two outermost lines; moving the taller line inward can never increase the area.",
    "Keep indices at both ends. Compute the area, then move the pointer at the shorter line inward, updating the best area at every step."
  ],
  "solution": "Hold two pointers at the ends of the list. The water height is the shorter line, so moving the taller line can never help; move the shorter line inward each step and track the largest distance-times-height seen. One pass: O(n) time, O(1) space.",
  "solutionCode": "def max_area(heights):\n    best = 0\n    left = 0\n    right = len(heights) - 1\n    while left < right:\n        height = min(heights[left], heights[right])\n        area = (right - left) * height\n        if area > best:\n            best = area\n        if heights[left] < heights[right]:\n            left += 1\n        else:\n            right -= 1\n    return best\n",
  "bruteForceCode": "def max_area(heights):\n    best = 0\n    n = len(heights)\n    for i in range(n):\n        hi = heights[i]\n        for j in range(i + 1, n):\n            h = heights[j]\n            area = (j - i) * (hi if hi < h else h)\n            if area > best:\n                best = area\n    return best\n",
  "failureHint": "hidden tests include flat and strictly decreasing heights, short lines at the ends, and one long random list",
  "expectedComplexity": { "time": "O(n)", "space": "O(1)" },
